---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import ModelCard from '../../../components/ModelCard.astro';
import { STANDARDS, RELATIONSHIP, MANUFACTURER } from '../../../data/site';
import { batteryModels } from '../../../data/battery-models';

const title = 'OEM Battery Specification Guide | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'A buyer’s guide to OEM battery specifications: standard family, voltage, capacity, CCA, dimensions, terminal, polarity and how to confirm values.';
const articleTitle = 'OEM battery specification guide';
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge</span>
    <h1>OEM battery specification guide</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-01 · Source: Chengguang Energy technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">A battery specification is only as useful as the fields you fix in advance. This guide explains each field buyers should confirm.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> A usable OEM battery specification fixes the standard family, voltage, capacity, CCA with its test basis, dimensions, terminal type and polarity. Never compare CCA values without stating whether they are JIS (−15°C) or EN (−18°C).
    </div>

    <h2>Standard family</h2>
    <p>Start with the standard your vehicles use: {STANDARDS.join(', ')}. JIS models such as 65D26 and DIN/EN models such as H6 describe different naming and test conventions.</p>

    <h2>Voltage</h2>
    <p>Passenger-car starter batteries are usually 12V; heavy-duty applications can also use 6V. Confirm the system voltage of the target vehicle.</p>

    <h2>Capacity (Ah)</h2>
    <p>Capacity describes the battery&rsquo;s energy content over a defined discharge test. It must be read with the standard because the same physical size can carry different ratings across families.</p>

    <h2>CCA</h2>
    <p>Cold cranking amps indicate starting performance at low temperature. CCA values are test-method dependent and must carry a reference basis — for example JIS (-15&deg;C) or EN (-18&deg;C). Always confirm final CCA with the manufacturer before ordering.</p>

    <h2>Dimensions, terminal and polarity</h2>
    <p>Fix length &times; width &times; height in millimetres, terminal type and polarity. If a field is not yet known, mark it &ldquo;confirm on inquiry&rdquo; rather than guessing.</p>

    <h2>Reference models</h2>
    <div class="model-list">
      {batteryModels.map((model) => <ModelCard model={model} />)}
    </div>
    <p>Specifications above are for reference; confirm final values with the manufacturer. <a href="/tools/battery-finder/">Use the battery finder &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>Dingwei Battery view</h2>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP} {MANUFACTURER.name} manufactures to JIS, DIN, BCI, AS and SA standards.</p>
    <p><a href="/oem/">Start an OEM program &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>CCA: two numbers for one battery</h2>
    <p>Cold cranking ampere values only compare when the test method is stated. The same battery can carry a lower JIS number and a higher EN / SAE number because each method uses a different test condition.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Test method</th><th>Temperature</th><th>Note</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>JIS</td><td>−15&deg;C</td><td>Lower number; common in Asia</td></tr>
        <tr><td>EN / SAE</td><td>−18&deg;C</td><td>Higher number; international</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>These are not directly convertible. Always state the test method next to a CCA value when issuing an RFQ.</p>

    <h2>The specification fields that actually matter</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Standard family and model reference</li>
      <li>Voltage and capacity</li>
      <li>CCA with its test basis</li>
      <li>Case size and terminal type</li>
      <li>Polarity</li>
      <li>Label and packaging requirements</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Put those fields into the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">OEM battery RFQ guide</a>, then check the responses against the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">supplier checklist</a>.</p>

    <h2>Specification drift: the hidden risk</h2>
    <p>Specification drift happens quietly on a repeat order: the approved sample is replaced by a different grid alloy, a shorter curing time, or an unapproved terminal change. The buyer only notices when the new container tests differently from the first one.</p>
    <p>The only durable control is to lock the approved specification and require formal change control for any deviation. Put that control in writing using the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">supplier checklist</a> before production restarts.</p>

    <h2>CCA: why one battery has two numbers</h2>
    <p>A single battery can show two CCA values because JIS (&minus;15&deg;C) and EN / SAE (&minus;18&deg;C) are different test methods, not a &ldquo;high&rdquo; and a &ldquo;low&rdquo; rating. If the RFQ does not state the test method, two suppliers can appear to be quoting different batteries when they are quoting the same product on different bases.</p>
    <p>State the basis on every line and compare only same-method values. See how that instruction fits into the wider request in the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">OEM battery RFQ guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <CTASection title="Finalize your battery specification" />
</ArticleLayout>
